※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=67 diag=none
※ pl4g-test: args --stack-size=128k --guard-size=8k

※ The same program on a stack of the size it was built with.
※
※ How large the stack is and how wide the guard below it is are decided when
※ the program is compiled, not when it runs: `--stack-size` and `--guard-size`,
※ which take a number of bytes with an optional K, M or G on the end.  A
※ generator emitting a program knows how deep it goes, and a program that knows
※ it needs little has no reason to reserve a megabyte.
※
※ What this shows is that the smaller stack is the one the program gets and
※ that the guard below it still catches the overflow: an eighth of the default
※ with an eighth of the default guard, and a call too deep still ends with the
※ status reserved for it rather than with a signal.  How far the recursion gets
※ before that happens is a fact about frame sizes and is not stated here.
※
※ Compare: **Go**'s -ldflags=-stacksize and its runtime maximum; **Rust**'s
※ RUST_MIN_STACK, read when a thread starts rather than when it is built;
※ **C**, where the size belongs to the loader and `ulimit -s` sets it; **Zig**
※ and **Odin**, which say it per thread at the point one is made.

fn deep(n: u64) → u64:
    if n = 0u64:
        0u64
    else:
        n + deep(n + 1u64)

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    ⎕narrow(deep(1u64), ⌜u6⌝) ?? 0u6
